<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Emar</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1185 BCE (approx.) · capital Emar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Emar, a Syrian trading city at the great bend of the Euphrates whose Late Bronze Age tablets show daily life and ritual under Hittite overlordship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Emar is named in the archives of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ebla">Ebla</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> as a market town where caravans from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> met boats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its position at the river's westward bend made it a key stage on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from the mid-3rd millennium BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 14th century BCE the city was rebuilt on a new site as the capital of the land of Ashtata, governed by local kings and a council of elders under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-carchemish">Viceroyalty of Carchemish</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>. Over a thousand Akkadian tablets record land sales, adoptions, wills and the festivals of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Emar was destroyed c. 1185 BCE in the wave of violence that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. Its site at Meskene was partly flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-assad">Lake Assad</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabqa-dam">Tabqa Dam</a>, and French salvage excavations in the 1970s recovered its archives.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emar.jpg" alt="Emar" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: James Gordon from Los Angeles, California, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_Byzantine_Tower_at_Meskene,_ancient_Barbalissos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1185 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Emar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Ashtata, Tell Meskene</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1842&amp;focus=emar" aria-label="Emar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 879 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5961 1135L5996 1079M5961 1135L5954 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5996 1079h0M5954 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5961 1135h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5961 1135h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1842&amp;focus=emar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

